    "abstract": "Traditional digital and analog computational architectures encounter thermodynamic dis- sipation limits dictated by Landauer’s principle, where logically irreversible bit erasure dis- sipates at least kBT ln 2 of thermal energy into the environment. While Charles Bennett’s foundational theorem proves that logically reversible operations can theoretically proceed without thermodynamic erasure (∆Slogical = 0), numerical implementations on classical hard- ware encounter floating-point truncation errors (IEEE 754 drift) that break exact unitarity (U †U̸ = I), re-introducing entropy degradation. In this work, we present a mathematically rigorous computational framework for exact, drift-free reversible state processing over the real quadratic number field extension Q( √ 5) ∼= Q(φ), where φ = 1+ √ 5 2 . By replacing ap- proximate floating-point representations with exact rational pairs (a, b) ∈Q2 and enforcing linear state transitions through algebraic Householder reflection matrices H = I −2 vv† v†v, we establish an isometric, bijective involution (H2 = I, H†H = I) with exact closure. We prove that the quantum density matrix trace remains strictly invariant (Tr(ρ) ≡1) and the von Neu- mann entropy change is identically zero (∆S ≡0) across arbitrarily long iterative operational cycles. A complete, un-simulated Python 3.9+ verification suite executing exact arbitrary- precision arithmetic over Q(φ) and auditing 10,000 reversible reflection cycles against IEEE 754 floating-point degradation is appended.",
