          "Theorem 1.2 (Bennett’s Reversible Computation Theorem (1973)). Every general-purpose classi- cal computation can be executed entirely through bijective, logically reversible operations. If an au- tomaton performs transitions such that every internal state has a unique predecessor and a unique 1",
          "successor, no logical information is erased, and the theoretical limit on thermodynamic energy dissi- pation is zero: ∆Qlogical = 0, ∆Slogical = 0 (2) 1.1 The Floating-Point Degradation Obstacle When reversible unitary transformations U ∈U(n) are implemented on physical hardware using standard IEEE 754 floating-point arithmetic (single or double precision ‘float64‘), floating-point non-associativity: (a + b) + c̸ = a + (b + c) (3) and round-off error introduce non-unitary deviations δU = Ufloat −U. Over N sequential itera- tions, this numerical degradation accumulates: ∥UN float(UN float)† −I∥F ∼N · ϵmach (4) This drift breaks bijectivity. The map ceases to be an exact isometry, artificially generating al- gorithmic entropy and rendering state recovery impossible over long execution horizons. To realize true zero-drift reversibility, computational state spaces must be formulated within an exact algebraic number field."
