          "Let V = Q(φ)n be an n-dimensional vector space over the field Q(φ). We equip V with the standard Euclidean inner product ⟨u, v⟩= Pn i=1 uivi.",
          "Definition 3.1 (Algebraic Householder Reflection Operator). Let v ∈Q(φ)n \\ {0} be a non-zero vector. The Householder reflection operator H(v) ∈Mn(Q(φ)) is defined by: H(v) := In −2vvT vT v (12) where vT v = Pn i=1 v2 i ∈Q(φ) \\ {0}.",
          "Theorem 3.2 (Involution, Symmetry, and Unitarity). The Householder matrix H(v) satisfies: 1. Exact Symmetry: H(v)T = H(v). 2. Exact Involution: H(v)2 = In. 3. Exact Orthogonality and Unitarity: H(v)†H(v) = H(v)T H(v) = In. Every matrix entry Hij belongs strictly to Q(φ).",
          "Proof. By definition: H(v)T = \u0012 In −2vvT vT v \u0013T = IT n −2(vvT )T vT v = In −2vvT vT v = H(v) (13) Evaluating the operator product H(v)2: H(v)H(v) = \u0012 In −2vvT vT v \u0013 \u0012 In −2vvT vT v \u0013 (14) = In −4vvT vT v + 4v(vT v)vT (vT v)2 (15) = In −4vvT vT v + 4vvT vT v = In (16) Because H(v)T = H(v) and H(v)2 = In, it follows that H(v)T H(v) = In. Thus H(v) is an exact orthogonal isometric involution."
