          "Let S(H) denote the convex space of physical density operators over an n-dimensional Hilbert space H ∼= Cn. A valid quantum state ρ ∈S(H) satisfies: ρ = ρ†, ρ ≥0, Tr(ρ) = 1 (17) When the density matrix is embedded in the field extension Mn(Q(φ)), state evolution under the Householder operator proceeds via unitary congruence: ρ(t + 1) = H(v)ρ(t)H(v)† = H(v)ρ(t)H(v) (18)",
          "Theorem 4.1 (Strict Trace Invariance and Isometry). Under arbitrary repeated applications of the transformation H(v), the density operator satisfies: Tr(ρ(N)) = Tr(ρ(0)) ≡1 ∀N ∈N (19) with identically zero numerical drift: ∥Tr(ρ(N)) −1∥Q = 0. 3",
          "Proof. Using the cyclic property of the trace operator: Tr(ρ(t + 1)) = Tr(H(v)ρ(t)H(v)) = Tr(H(v)2ρ(t)) (20) By Theorem 3.2, H(v)2 = In. Therefore: Tr(H(v)2ρ(t)) = Tr(Inρ(t)) = Tr(ρ(t)) (21) By induction, Tr(ρ(N)) = Tr(ρ(0)) = 1 for all N ≥1.",
          "Theorem 4.2 (Zero von Neumann Entropy Generation). The von Neumann entropy S(ρ) = −kBTr(ρ ln ρ) is strictly invariant under the transformation H(v): ∆S = S(ρ(N)) −S(ρ(0)) ≡0 (22) The operation is strictly adiabatic and logically reversible, satisfying Bennett’s thermodynamic condi- tion.",
          "Proof. Let ρ(0) = P i λi|i⟩⟨i| be the spectral decomposition of ρ(0). Under the transformation H(v), the transformed density operator is: ρ(N) = H(v)Nρ(0)(H(v)N)† = X i λi \u0000H(v)N|i⟩ \u0001 \u0010 ⟨i|(H(v)N)†\u0011 (23) Because H(v)N is unitary, the set {H(v)N|i⟩} forms an orthonormal basis. Thus, the eigenvalues {λi} of ρ(N) are identical to those of ρ(0). Since the von Neumann entropy depends solely on the eigenvalue spectrum: S(ρ(N)) = −kB X i λi ln λi = S(ρ(0)) (24) which implies ∆S = 0 identically."
